{
  "experiment": "M1_target_race",
  "question": "Under N concurrent set-target requests, does exactly one is_current=true row survive, and how do naive vs. RPC paths differ?",
  "runs": [
    {
      "concurrency": 5,
      "naive": {
        "ok": 1,
        "failed": 4,
        "error_types": {
          "duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 4
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      },
      "rpc": {
        "ok": 1,
        "failed": 4,
        "error_types": {
          "Failed to set contributor target: duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 4
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "concurrency": 25,
      "naive": {
        "ok": 2,
        "failed": 23,
        "error_types": {
          "duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 23
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      },
      "rpc": {
        "ok": 6,
        "failed": 19,
        "error_types": {
          "Failed to set contributor target: duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 19
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "concurrency": 50,
      "naive": {
        "ok": 2,
        "failed": 48,
        "error_types": {
          "duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 48
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      },
      "rpc": {
        "ok": 11,
        "failed": 39,
        "error_types": {
          "Failed to set contributor target: duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"idx_targets_current_event\"": 39
        },
        "current_targets_after": 1
      }
    }
  ],
  "invariant_current_targets_always_1": true,
  "finding": "Invariant held at every concurrency level for both paths (current_targets_after === 1 always). However, the RPC path still rejects most concurrent requests with a raw duplicate-key error (see rpc.error_types) rather than serializing them gracefully, because its SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks the *existing* current-target row; once the lock holder commits and supersedes that row, a transaction that was blocked on it re-checks `WHERE is_current = true` under READ COMMITTED, finds no match (the row the lock was on is no longer current), treats it as \"no previous target\", and attempts a fresh INSERT that collides with whichever transaction already won — causing a duplicate-key exception instead of a clean retry/serialize. The outcome is always correct (never >1 current target), but under concurrent load most competing requests surface as hard errors to the caller rather than succeeding in turn."
}
